  ### Package Management & Configuration                                                                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### requirements.txt                                                                                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: This is the authoritative shopping list of third-party Python libraries needed to run the system: PyMuPDF for reading PDF boxes, Pillow for image conversion, Pydantic for data      
  validation, LanceDB for vectors, Streamlit for the UI, and Pytest for testing.                                                                                                                       
  • Why it’s a separate file: Standard Python convention. It allows anyone cloning the repo to get up and running with a single environment installation command.                                      
  • What breaks if deleted: Anyone trying to replicate or set up your virtual environment has to guess which versions of which libraries were installed.                                               
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### pyproject.toml                                                                                                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: Makes your custom code under the source directory installable as an actual Python package. It lets any script cleanly import components without awkward folder-traversal hacks.      
  • Why it’s a separate file: Python's modern packaging standards require this file to declare editable package metadata and command-line entry points.                                                
  • What breaks if deleted: Running package installation fails, the command-line shortcut stops existing, and internal imports across layout, extraction, and database modules will immediately throw  
  module-not-found errors.                                                                                                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### .gitignore                                                                                                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: Tells Git what never to track. Crucially, it stops your secret environment keys, temporary cache folders, compiled Python files, and the giant 108 MB PDF from ever being committed  
  to GitHub.                                                                                                                                                                                           
  • Why it’s a separate file: Git natively requires this at the root of a workspace.                                                                                                                   
  • What breaks if deleted: Your next Git commit will attempt to upload a massive 108 MB PDF, your private API tokens, and thousands of virtual environment files to your Git host, likely exceeding   
  GitHub's file size limit and leaking credentials.                                                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### .env.example & .env                                                                                                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: .env.example is an empty template showing what environment variables the system looks for (confidence thresholds, model names, API keys). .env is your actual private file where real
  keys live.                                                                                                                                                                                           
  • Why it’s a separate file: Secret hygiene. You keep .env out of version control and commit .env.example so other collaborators know what keys are needed without exposing yours.                    
  • What breaks if deleted: If .env.example is deleted, teammates won't know what variables to configure. If .env is deleted, the pipeline will fall back to built-in defaults or complain when        
  reaching steps that require external API keys.                                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### README.md                                                                                                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: The welcome mat and quickstart handbook. It explains what the project does, shows how to activate the environment on Windows, and lists the exact terminal commands to run each step.
  • Why it’s a separate file: Universal standard for software repositories.                                                                                                                            
  • What breaks if deleted: Anyone opening the project on GitHub or locally has no quick overview of how to install, test, or run it.                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  #### Makefile                                                                                                                                                                                        
                                                                                                                                                                                                       
  • What it does: A shortcut runner. Instead of typing long commands to run tests, launch the UI, or execute the pipeline on sample pages, you can type short targets like make run or make test.      
  • Why it’s a separate file: It decouples human memory from command syntax.                                                                                                                           
  • What breaks if deleted: Nothing in Python breaks; you just have to type the full command strings into PowerShell yourself.                                                                         
